# ALL.mask -- CEO-409 line masks for the csnobol4 (Budne) suite, declared beside the data.
# FORMAT: entry<TAB>python-regex<TAB>reason.  A row with an empty reason is REFUSED by the harness.
#
# ✅ IN EFFECT SINCE 2026-09-08 (hq_T, ceo CEO-432 item 1). This header said NOT YET IN EFFECT and it was
# right to: test_snobol4_csnobol4_suite.sh grades with its own loop and read no mask sidecar, so these two
# rows were inert -- and saying so in the file is why the gap was closeable instead of discoverable. The
# runner now reaches the harness's OWN reader through scripts/util_apply_ceo409_mask.py (one dialect, one
# path rule, one refusal), and the `*` entry column is resolved by masks_for() so both readers agree by
# construction. MEASURED ON THE LANDING RUN: 44 masked lines across 22 fixtures, two each -- and the board
# did not move, 66 PASS / 21 FAIL / 6 REJECT both modes before and after, because those 22 are red for the
# other reasons this header already named. The mechanism is live, audited, and hides nothing today, which
# is the only state in which a mask mechanism should ever be handed over.
#
# ⭐ EARNED UNDER CEO-420 CLAUSE (b), NOT CLAUSE (a), AND CLAUSE (a) WAS MEASURED AND FAILED FIRST.
# Clause (a), NONDETERMINISM -- the oracle's own value moves between runs -- DOES NOT HOLD here: five
# consecutive `sbl -bf rewind1.sno < <its stdin>` runs give `memory used (bytes) 11416` and
# `memory left (bytes) 1037152` byte-identically, and -s2m, -s8m and -m2m do not shift either number
# (hq_R, 2026-09-08). Determinism is why this needed clause (b) and why the original earning condition
# was amended: a number can be perfectly reproducible by ONE engine and unreachable by every other.
#
# ⭐ CLAUSE (b) DEMONSTRATION, IMPLEMENTATION-INTERNAL BY CONSTRUCTION, measured not asserted (hq_R,
# 2026-09-08). Two programs whose PROGRAM OUTPUT is byte-identical -- both print exactly `same` -- and
# whose values in this field differ:
#     A:  OUTPUT = 'same' ; REWIND(13)                      -> memory used 11320 · memory left 1037248
#     B:  JUNK = DUPL('x',5000) ; OUTPUT = 'same' ; REWIND(13) -> memory used 16456 · memory left 1032112
# Same output, different field. The quantity therefore belongs to the ENGINE's bookkeeping and not to the
# program's data, which is what clause (b) requires and what clause (4) of the ceiling requires it not to
# violate. `sbl -bf`, /home/resources/x64/bin/sbl.
#
# ⛔ THE CEILING STILL BINDS AND IS NOT SATISFIED BY THIS FILE ALONE: masked lines are COUNTED AND PRINTED
# beside the board, and if the masked lines are a MAJORITY of a fixture's divergence that fixture is
# ungradable and goes outside the baseline instead, NAMED. For several of these programs the majority test
# will decide against the mask on other grounds entirely -- the coo measured that the oracle refuses them
# for its own reasons (ERROR 022 undefined function x7, ERROR 116/160 file specification x5, ERROR 251
# undefined keyword x3) and that `digits` would have to REGRESS to go green, because SPITBOL has no
# &DIGITS and we do. A mask is not a route around that; it only ever covers these two lines.
*	^memory used \(bytes\)  ([0-9]+|-)$	SPITBOL's own allocator arithmetic: a quantity of the engine's heap bookkeeping, not of the program's data. Deterministic per program (five identical runs; unmoved by -s2m/-s8m/-m2m) and therefore earned under CEO-420 clause (b), not clause (a): two programs with byte-identical output read 11320 and 16456. No correct implementation can produce SPITBOL's figure by computing anything, only by transcribing it. CEO-1344: SCRIP's rendered fatal block carries a dash where it carries no figure, so the placeholder lands on both sides.
*	^memory left \(bytes\)  ([0-9]+|-)$	The same allocator arithmetic from the other side, and the same demonstration: 1037248 vs 1032112 for two programs whose output is byte-identical. Masked with its twin or not at all -- masking one and grading the other would grade the arena size, which is neither engine's answer to anything the program asked. CEO-1344: the rendered block's dash is admitted.
*	^stmts executed       ([0-9]+|-)$	CEO-1344 (a SPITBOL fatal is gradable, the coo 2026-09-28): SPITBOL's own statement counter, printed in its fatal block -- oracle-internal until SCRIP reports its own count; SCRIP's rendered block carries a dash.
*	^execution time msec  ([0-9]+|-)$	CEO-1344: SPITBOL's own clock reading in its fatal block, a duration and never a program's answer; SCRIP's rendered block carries a dash.
*	^REGENERATIONS        ([0-9]+|-)$	CEO-1344: SPITBOL's own collector count in its fatal block, engine bookkeeping and not the program's data; SCRIP's rendered block carries a dash.
diag1	^&ERRLIMIT = \d+$	CEO-1293 kept enhancement, Lon: "Keep the VALUE function we like new features." (CEO-1507 restores it) -- diag1 line &ERRLIMIT: &ERRLIMIT counts down once per trapped error, and the two VALUE('b') calls (source lines 175 and 181) are each one trap in sbl -bf, which has no VALUE, and none here (998 vs 1000)
diag1	^&ERRTEXT = '.*'$	CEO-1293 kept enhancement, Lon: "Keep the VALUE function we like new features." (CEO-1507 restores it) -- diag1 line &ERRTEXT: &ERRTEXT holds the text of the last trapped error: sbl -bf's is 'undefined function called' from its VALUE('b') trap (source lines 175 and 181), ours is empty because VALUE is defined here
diag1	^&ERRTYPE = \d+$	CEO-1293 kept enhancement, Lon: "Keep the VALUE function we like new features." (CEO-1507 restores it) -- diag1 line &ERRTYPE: &ERRTYPE holds the number of the last trapped error: sbl -bf's is 22 from its VALUE('b') trap (source lines 175 and 181), ours is 0 because VALUE is defined here
diag1	^&STCOUNT = \d+$	CEO-1293 kept enhancement, Lon: "Keep the VALUE function we like new features." (CEO-1507 restores it) -- diag1 line &STCOUNT: &STCOUNT counts the statements executed, and the two VALUE('b') traps of source lines 175 and 181 move the count by one in sbl -bf against ours (268 vs 267)
